  "updated_at": "2026-08-10T08:53:21.990Z",
  "description": "An ultra-low-power machine-vision tile built around the PixArt PAG7920J3, a 320 × 240 monochrome global-shutter camera module with an integrated lens. It captures 8-bit RAW frames at up to 180 fps over SPI, with 3.0 µm pixels, a 71.3° × 53.7° field of view, and 64 dB of dynamic range. Configuration is over I2C. A proprietary on-chip motion-detection mode draws under 100 µW (compared to 760 µW for QVGA output at 15 fps) making the tile practical to leave always-on in a battery-powered system, with auto exposure and gain control, auto black cancellation, mirror/flip, windowing, LED strobe, and multi-sensor frame sync built in.\n\n",
  "json_version": "0.7",
  "application_notes": [
    {
      "sort": 0,
      "details": "when GPIO2 (pad 3) is left floating, the I2C address will default to 0x35.  Pulling GPIO2 (pad 3) high will change the address to 0x25, while grounding pad 3 will set it to 0x40.  The camera will also respond to general broadcasts on 0x60.",
      "heading": "I2C address",
